A physical charged particle is not merely a bare charge. Its state is a quantum superposition containing a bare electron component, an electron with one photon, an electron with two photons, and so on. In deliberately schematic notation, $\begin{aligned}
\ket{e_{\mathrm{phys}}}
={}&a_0\ket e
+a_1\ket{e+\text{one photon}}
\\
&+a_2\ket{e+\text{two photons}}+\cdots .
\end{aligned}$ This notation records only the hierarchy of photon-number components; it does not specify photon momenta or attempt a complete construction of an interacting charged state. The surrounding photons are emitted and reabsorbed rather than escaping as free radiation. Their effects can be measured, although they are not detected in the same way as freely propagating photons. Put a second charge nearby and a photon emitted at one charge can be absorbed at the other. The two dressed states then cease to have an energy equal to the simple sum of two isolated energies.

